  "worked_solution": "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nOur relation is defined on $\\{\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\delta ,\\gamma \\},$ and has no pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y.$ As a result, the propositions in both implications above are false. Hence, the implications themselves must be true. Therefore, since for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)\\in R & \\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R, \\\\ (x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R & \\,⟹\\,x=y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe relation is $\\text{both symmetric and antisymmetric}.$",
